erosis  and waited to see how severe  the disease was in these animals  and the mice that got eie who had  no bugs in their intestine that's the  brown line  they actually had a milder disease  course  than the ones who got bugs from  healthy individuals which was more the  control  so the healthy individuals are in the  blue line and that's more your quote  unquote  normal expected rate of ea in this  particular mouse strain  the mice who got a fecal transplant from  ms patients had more severe eae had more  severe disease  ms life disease than the control animals  and so that is  potentially very important  likewise the  immune cells in these animals who had  been transplanted  varied and you can see in the graph that  the control animals had  normal levels of regulatory t cells  whereas the animals who'd been  transplanted with  ms fecal microbes did not  they're they're in the red bar and that  indicates that their  uh regulatory t-cells were not  functioning in a regulatory capacity  and if you'll recall that is similar to  what's seen  in multiple sclerosis patients which we  went over  a couple slides ago this right  dysregulated  t-reg or regulatory t cell response  so we can say that the immune  sorry that the microbiome appears to be  dysregulated  in a mess and that  it's likely based on animal studies that  this dysregulation can have  implications for the severity of their  disease  but human research is tougher than mouse  research in a lot of different ways  and human microbiome research is  particularly challenging  in a lot of aspects so how do we learn  about the human microbiome  well first off we have to recognize that  humans  are not identical this is one of the  beauties of animal research you can  assume that your mice  mouse one is essentially equal to ml3  and l4  within a strain they're genetically  effectively identical  that's not the case with humans one  they're not genetically identical  two they have different environments  they take