y different um and  so the fact that one's studies saw  decreased bacteroides and another saw  increased bacteroides  isn't necessarily unsurprising in a way  but this does make it difficult for us  to try and dial down to what's the core  truth what is  the state of the microbiome in ms and  some of that we may be able to learn a  little bit better based on improvements  in sequencing platforms  so i talked to you a lot about the short  amplicon  sequencing for 16s and most of that  sequencing relies on the me seeker  illumina platform  this platform can sequence amplicons  that are up to around 300  at a good day probably 250 more  realistically  base pairs at a time and more  third generation sequencing technologies  have changed the  chemistry in such that these pac bio  platform  is now able to sequence 10 kilobytes at  a time so much much longer amplicons  and the use of this platform has enabled  improvements in the 16s technology so  that we're no longer sequencing  just the v4 region for example  so long amplicon sequencing involving  the entire 16s  the internally transcribed spacer and  the part of the 23s  gene is what my group is using and this  methodology  is significantly more powerful than the  short amplicon sequencing and that it is  able to achieve species  and often strain level resolution  um there are certain bugs that it won't  pick up  helicobacter and archaea species for  example  but overall this is a much finer  granularity and allows more detailed  taxonomy than any of the uh  short amplicon sequencing so our  pipeline is to collect stool  at home we also collected detailed  metadata  as i mentioned earlier this is a very  important consideration in human  microbiome research we have it shipped  overnight to our lab  and immediately aliquot and then when  we're ready to process we do so in  batches  isolate the dna achieve the sequences  de-multiplex the sequences  we use data2 to denoise the data and  then align against  an athena database that's a modified  version  that includes the internally transcribed  spacer and the 23s gene as well  um just a short look at our demographics  i'm going to summarize this to say that  we have  a little over 30 ms patients and we have  longitudinal samples on most of these  patients both at baseline and then after  they start immunomodulatory therapy  and we have healthy controls to match  them  and we've collected detailed information  about these patients their diets their  stool habits their travel history  just everything we could think of that  might potentially impact the microbiome  we um again as i showed you our pipeline  just looking at the mock community reads  we're just demonstrating quality  of this pipeline you can see that before  the dota 2  denoising step we do have a lot of read  counts which gets decreased as expected  when um the  errors are filtered out with the data 2  stuff and  the important things to note is that  the post dota 2  sequences that have more than 10  aggregate reads over the course of the  entire data set  is almost identical to that that's  expected based on manufacturer provided  specs about the mock communities  and that after data 2 we have  essentially 100 of our reads are  assigned to the expected species so we  feel very confident this  this is a high quality way of  analyzing these data we then are going  to come back to  our uh our specimens and i'm just going  to take just a quick moment to talk  about ways of measuring the microbiome  we look often at alpha and beta  diversity so alpha diversity refers to  diversity within a sample  how many species can be detected within  a given specimen  you can also talk about beta diversity  which is diversity between  samples so the  top set of circles has a  high beta diversity because many species  are different between  these two organisms or these two um  sets of of data whereas the lower  circle would refer to a low beta  diversity even though  there's a lot of different bugs in that  lower right circle  um they're almost all shared with the  circle on the left so the beta diversity  there would be lower  and in our data sets the alpha and beta  diversity of ms patients are  significantly  different compared to the healthy  controls overall the alpha diversity was  decreased amongst our ms patients  this was most pronounced before they  started immunomodulatory therapy  the beta diversity if anything was  increased in the beta sorry in the ms  patients at baseline  and we then wanted to look at the  overall underlying populations making up  this change in diversity  because the alpha and the beta diversity  tell us nothing about  the specific bugs only that there's a  difference  and we did a analysis of the composition  of microbiomes  which is a data technique that's  designed for microbiome research  looking at overall which sets of bugs  are different  and we identified bacteroides as  relatively enriched in patients who had  multiple sclerosis  so we then wanted to look at which  specific bugs were enriched  and we also wanted to examine the impact  of immunotherapy on the gut microbiome  so patients with  ms typically are treated with  immunotherapy and in our cohort these  are actually  all of these patients were treated with  b cell depleting immunotherapies  and many medications including some  other  immune modifying medications do impact  the composition of the gut microbiome  and we were curious whether that was  part of how these mechanisms were  sorry how these medications were acting  in ms  and so we looked at the species level  identification of these bacteroides  again this was identified at the genus  level as being  enriched in ms here you can see some of  the specific  species which appeared to be enriched in  the ms patients compared to the healthy  individuals  and interestingly you can see that the  most of these species appear to decrease  or the  the trends is more for  a distribution that more resembles the  healthy controls as the individuals are  treated with  immunomodulatory b-cell depleting  medications for longer periods of time  which is really interesting and we want  to flush this out further  so future directions for us is that we  are still increasing our cohort size and  the extent of our longitudinal follow-up  with these patients  i told you that we collected a lot of  clinical metadata into this and that  this  collection of clinical metadata is  extremely important for  human microbiome research and so we are  still incorporating these clinical  metadata into  our analysis we also  are planning to expand this  uh data set into multi-ohmic research  and look also at the metabolites  produced by these microbes and that will  be  yet another very important aspect of how  the microbiome is acting so it likely  won't be enough just to know which bugs  are there and which are not  we'll also need to know what they're  doing what are the  what are the metabolic implications or  the functional implications of this  change and this dysbiosis that we see in  autoimmune disease  so that is my talk for today  i'd like to give special acknowledgement  to bill ruff  upper right picture uh to  ganit janda and to ina cohen my students  who have just done  most of this work and it's a phenomenal  job from all three of them  really made this work possible i also  acknowledge david haffler my mentor  and his lab and my funding sources and  with that  i would like to thank you for your  attention any questions can be  directed towards me via the platform  thank you so much

