 cross  reactive with for example a myelin or a  nerve epitope  and the immune system develops a  response to the  microbe and as a side effect  recognizes the brain and causes damage  other  ways in which the microbiome can  contribute to autoimmunity it can just  regulate the barrier function between  the gut and the circulation it can alter  production of immunoregulatory molecules  so  specifically some bacteria produce  short-chain fatty acids  which can include butyrate and these are  known to be immunomodulatory  and promotes usually an  anti-inflammatory immune response  a tryptophan metabolites among others  are also known to have direct  impact on the circulating immune cells  and the  immune cells that are located in the gut  mucosa  and so this these are  various ways in which the microbiome can  lead to or contribute to  a dysregulated immune response and then  autoimmune immune response  so i'm going to go back to multiple  sclerosis now that's what we're  specifically talking about today  and a number of studies so far have  demonstrated that the gut microbiome is  abnormal  in multiple sclerosis and there have  been a handful of papers on the subject  all of which agree  that the gut microbiome is abnormal in  ms  but the specifics of how it's abnormal  vary with the study as you can see from  this table  there's been a number of bugs implicated  and they are not the same bugs from  study to study  i'm going to come back to this in just a  moment as to why this is  but suffice to say that there is  agreement that the gut microbiome  in ms does not look the same as the gut  microbiome in a healthy individual  and this appears to be functionally  important  some studies looking at this took  microbes  from individuals who had multiple  sclerosis  or healthy individuals and they  transplanted those  microbes into germ-free mice they then  gave the mice  experimental autoimmune  encephalomyelitis or eae  and that's the main animal model for  multiple sclerosis  and waited to see how severe  the disease was in these animals  and the mice that got eie who had  no bugs in their intestine that's the  brown line  they actually had a milder disease  course  than the ones who got bugs from  healthy individuals which was more the  control  so the healthy individuals are in the  blue line and that's more your quote  unquote  normal expected rate of ea in this  particular mouse strain  the mice who got a fecal transplant from  ms patients had more severe eae had more  severe disease  ms life disease than the control animals  and so that is  potentially very important  likewise the  immune cells in these animals who had  been transplanted  varied and you can see in the graph that  the control animals had  normal levels of regulatory t cells  whereas the animals who'd been  transplanted with  ms fecal microbes did not  they're they're in the red bar and that  indicates that their  uh regulatory t-cells were not  functioning in a regulatory capacity  and if you'll recall that is similar to  what's seen  in multiple sclerosis patients which we  went over  a couple slides ago this right  dysregulated  t-reg or regulatory t cell response  so we can say that the immune  sorry that the microbiome appears to be  dysregulated  in a mess and that  it's likely based on animal studies that  this dysregulation can have  implications for the severity of their  disease  but human research is tougher than mouse  research in a lot of different ways  and human microbiome research is  particularly challenging  in a lot of aspects so how do we learn  about the human microbiome  well first off we have to recognize that  humans  are not identical this is one of the  beauties of animal research you can  assume that your mice  mouse one is essentially equal to ml3  and l4  within a strain they're genetically  effectively identical  that's not the case with humans one  they're not genetically identical  two they have different environments  they take different medications  they eat different things they were born  in different ways  c-section versus vaginal growth for  example  and they have different habits so all of  these  variables are known to impact the  microbiome  and so any human microbiome study needs  to take into account these what we call  metadata these  clinical considerations um so  this is something that's very important  to consider the next  point with human microbiome research is  you need to decide which  site you want to study there are a lot  of different places where microbes live  in humans and we have to decide which is  the most important  i'm talking today about the gut  microbiome but there are also  plenty of studies on the skin microbiome  the oral microbiome  respiratory or genital urinary tract and  the specific research question that  you're interested in will drive which  site you're looking at  after you've characterized your patients  you've decided which aspect of the  microbiome you're going to study  then you have to decide your tool and  the tools for microbiome study  are many and have changed over the years  so just talking a little bit about the  tools for  microbiol microbial taxonomy which is  the most  common way that people study the  microbiome  older studies were limited to things  that could be cultured and that was very  limited as you can imagine not every bug  that lives in the intestine is rather  readily culturable for sequencing  technology 16s  rrna emerged as the main  way in which we understand the taxonomy  of these bacteria  the 16s rrna gene is evolutionarily  conserved among  bacterial species and so um sequencing  just this one gene or even part of that  gene  can lead to taxonomic identification of  your bugs and so  initially people did full length 16s  sequencing which took a long time  cost quite a lot of money it was very  slow um  this evolved into short amplicon  sequencing and we'll get  into that just in a moment and has  evolved from there  so 16 as our rna sequencing of specific  hypervariable regions is the standard  for  16s taxonomy based methods  of characterizing the microbiome and the  16s  our rna gene has nine variable regions  b1 through v9  and one of the things that needs to be  understood about these is that not every  type of short amplicon 16s rna  sequencing is going to give you the same  data  so certain of the hypervariable regions  are more highly evolutionarily conserved  and others  mutate more frequently the v4 through v6  regions are the most conserved  evolutionarily  and the before region is the most  similar  to the average 16s rrna gene  when you sequence the whole thing  however  v4 sequencing typically cannot achieve  fine detail in terms of the bacterial  taxonomy  so you are limited to family order  genus level identification and  you really can't get to species and  strain level identification with  v4 sequencing if you  include the v2 and v8 regions you may be  able to get a little bit more  fine detail on the taxonomy but then  they run into other  problems with those areas and so  i've just outlined here in this slide a  couple of the  benefits and fallbacks of each  very hypervariable region and you see  that most of them  have preferences for certain types of  bugs that live are more easily  identified in certain areas  and so these are the choice of region  is is important when considering what  your  tool is that you're going to study the  microbiome with  so in terms of ms the existing data are  limited  in a lot of different ways the patient  cohorts have been very heterogeneous  the sequencing methodologies have been  variable  and we've looked primarily at taxonomy  not at bacterial function  so if i go back to this table that i  showed you before  and it said you know this is this looks  very different from study to study  well that could be because we're  comparing apples and oranges  the methodology methodologies used for  each of these  studies was very different um and  so the fact that one's studies saw  decreased bacteroides and another saw  increased bacteroides  isn't necessarily unsurprising in a way  but this does make it difficult for us  to try and dial down to what's the core  truth what is  the state of the microbiome in ms and  some of that we may be able to learn a  little bit better based on improvements  in sequencing platforms  so i talked to you a lot about the short  amplicon  sequencing for 16s and most of that  sequencing relies on the me seeker  illumina platform  this platform can sequence amplicons  that are up to around 300  at a good day probably 250 more  realistically  base pairs at a time and more  third generation sequencing technologies  have changed the  chemistry in such that these pac bio  platform  is now able to sequence 10 kilobytes at  a time so much much longer amplicons  and the use of this platform has enabled  improvements in the 16s technology so  that we're no longer sequencing  just the v4 region for example  so long amplicon sequencing involving  the entire 16s  the internally transcribed spacer and  the part of the 23s  gene is what my group is using and this  methodology  is significantly more powerful than the  short amplicon sequencing and that it is  able to achieve species  and often strain level resolution  um there are certain bugs that it won't  pick up  helicobacter and archaea species for  example  but overall this is a much finer  granularity and allows more detailed  taxonomy than any of the uh  short amplicon sequencing so our  pipeline is to collect stool  at home we also collected detailed  metadata  as i mentioned earlier this is a very  important consideration in human  microbiome research we have it shipped  overnight to our lab  and immediately aliquot and then when  we're ready to process we do so in  batches  isolate the dna achieve the sequences  de-multiplex the sequences  we use data2 to denoise the data and  then align against  an athena database that's a modified  version  that includes the internally transcribed  spacer and the 23s gene as well  um just a short look at our demographics  i'm going to summarize this to say that  we have  a little over 30 ms patients and we have  longitudinal samples on most of these  patients both at baseline and then after  they start immunomodulatory therapy  and we have healthy controls to match  them  and we've collected detailed information  about these patients their diets their  stool habits their travel history  just everything we could think of that  might potentially impact the microbiome  we um again as i showed you our pipeline  just looking at the mock community reads  we're just demonstrating quality  of this pipeline you can see that before  the dota 2  denoising step we do have a lot of read  counts which gets decreased as expected  when um the  errors are filtered out with the data 2  stuff and  the important things to note is that  the post dota 2  sequences that have more than 10  aggregate reads over the course of the  entire data set  is almost identical to that that's  expected based on manufacturer provided  specs about the mock communities  and that after data 2 we have  essentially 100 of our reads are  assigned to the expected species so we  feel very confident this  this is a high quality way of  analyzing these data we then are going  to come back to  our uh our specimens and i'm just going  to take just a quick moment to talk  about ways of measuring the microbiome  we look often at alpha and beta  diversity so alpha diversity refers to  diversity within a sample  how many species can be detected within  a given specimen  you can also talk about beta diversity  which is diversity between  samples so the  top set of circles has a  high beta diversity because many species  are different between  these two organisms or these two um  sets of of data whereas the lower  circle would refer to a low beta  diversity even though  there's a lot of different bugs in that  lower right circle  um they're almost all shared with the  circle on the left so the beta diversity  there would be lower  and in our data sets the alpha and beta  diversity of ms patients are  significantly  different compared to the healthy  controls overall the alpha diversity was  decreased amongst our ms patients  this was most pronounced before they  started immunomodulatory therapy  the beta diversity if anything was  increased in the beta sorry in the ms  patients at baseline  and we then wanted to look at the  overall underlying populations making up  this change in diversity  because the alpha and the beta diversity  tell us nothing about  the specific bugs only that there's a  difference  and we did a analysis of the composition  of microbiomes  which is a data technique that's  designed for microbiome research  looking at overall which sets of bugs  are different  and we identified bacteroides as  relatively enriched in patients who had  multiple sclerosis  so we then wanted to look at which  specific bugs were enriched  and we also wanted to examine the impact  of immunotherapy on the gut microbiome  so patients with  ms typically are treated with  immunotherapy and in our cohort these  are actually  all of these patients were treated with  b cell depleting immunotherapies  and many medications including some  other  immune modifying medications do impact  the composition of the gut microbiome  and we were curious whether that was  part of how these mechanisms were  sorry how these medications were acting  in ms  and so we looked at the species level  identification of these bacteroides  again this was identified at the genus  level as being  enriched in ms here you can see some of  the specific  species which appeared to be enriched in  the ms patients compared to the healthy  individuals  and interestingly you can see that the  most of these species appear to decrease  or the  the trends is more for  a distribution that more resembles the  healthy controls as the individuals are  treated with  immunomodulatory b-cell depleting  medications for longer periods of time  which is really interesting and we want  to flush this out further  so future directions for us is that we  are still increasing our cohort size and  the extent of our longitudinal follow-up  with these patients  i told you that we collected a lot of  clinical metadata into this and that  this  collection of clinical metadata is  extremely important for  human microbiome research and so we are  still incorporating these clinical  metadata into  our analysis we also  are planning to expand this  uh data set into multi-ohmic research  and look also at the metabolites  produced by these microbes and that will  be  yet another very important aspect of how  the microbiome is acting so it likely  won't be enough just to know which bugs  are there and which are not  we'll also need to know what they're  doing what are the  what are the metabolic implications or  the functional implications of this  change and this dysbiosis that we see in  autoimmune disease  so that is my talk for today  i'd like to give special acknowledgement  to bill ruff  upper right picture uh to  ganit janda and to ina cohen my students  who have just done  most of this work and it's a phenomenal  job from all three of them  really made this work possible i also  acknowledge david haffler my mentor  and his lab and my funding sources and  with that  i would like to thank you for your  attention any questions can be  directed towards me via the platform  thank you so much
 

